{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"77444724d4d02293","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A group $(G, \\cdot)$ is a pair where $G$ is a set and $\\cdot$ is a binary operation over $G$ satisfying:\n1) $\\exists e \\in G: \\forall a \\in G, \\enspace e \\cdot a = a \\cdot e = a$\n2) $\\forall a \\in G, \\enspace \\exists b \\in G : a \\cdot b = b \\cdot a = e$\n3) $\\forall a, b, c \\in G, \\enspace (a \\cdot b) \\cdot c = a \\cdot (b \\cdot c)$","x":202,"y":147,"width":478,"height":193},
		{"id":"ff2380dea8f90636","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. The unitary group of dimension $n$ is defined as\n$$U(n) = \\{U \\in \\mathbb{C}^{n \\times n}: U^\\dagger U = UU^\\dagger = I\\}$$","x":760,"y":460,"width":375,"height":136},
		{"id":"72778dace999579c","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. The special unitary group of dimension $n$ is defined as\n$$SU(n) = \\{U \\in U(n): \\text{det}(U) = 1\\}$$","x":728,"y":652,"width":440,"height":128},
		{"id":"f6b6f43564723f4b","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A metric space $(X, d)$ is a set $X$ and a function \n$$d: X \\times X \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}$$\nsatisfying\n1) $d(x, y) = 0 \\iff x = y$\n2) $d(x, y) = d(y, x)$\n3) $d(x, z) \\lt d(x, y) + d(y, z)$","x":-400,"y":180,"width":480,"height":240},
		{"id":"8e42d5e7353fc8d9","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A function $g$ is said to be $O(f)$ if, and only if\n$$\\exists c \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\exists n_0 \\in \\mathbb{N}: \\forall n \\gt n_0, \\enspace 0 \\leq g(n) \\leq c \\cdot f(n)$$","x":-940,"y":200,"width":457,"height":100},
		{"id":"49652abfbffffa80","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A subgroup $K$ of a group $(G, \\cdot)$, denoted $K \\leq G$ satisfies\n1) $K \\subseteq G$\n2) $(K, \\cdot)$ is a group","x":-160,"y":652,"width":377,"height":152},
		{"id":"aed1589db481fe57","type":"text","text":"**Definition**. Let $(H, \\cdot)$ be a group and $S$ a set. The subgroup generated by $S$, denoted $\\langle S \\rangle$ is defined as\n$$\\langle S \\rangle = \\bigcap_{S \\subseteq K \\leq H}K$$\nthat is, $\\langle S \\rangle$ is the smallest subgroup of $(H, \\cdot)$ containing $S$.","x":-211,"y":852,"width":479,"height":175},
		{"id":"01130acb20d645e3","x":348,"y":652,"width":314,"height":127,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. An abelian group $(G, \\cdot)$ is a group further satisfying\n1) $\\forall a, b \\in G, \\enspace a \\cdot b = b \\cdot a$"},
		{"id":"42ed3d4b39669217","x":320,"y":873,"width":460,"height":247,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A ring is a triple $(R, +, \\cdot)$ where $R$ is a set, and $+, \\cdot$ are binary operations satisfying\n1) $(R, +)$ is an abelian group\n2) $\\forall a, b, c \\in R, \\enspace (ab)c = a(bc)$\n3) $\\forall a, b, c \\in R, \\enspace a(b + c) = ab + ac$\n4) $\\forall a, b, c \\in R, \\enspace (a + b)c = ac + bc$\n5) $\\exists 1 \\in R : \\forall a \\in R, \\enspace a1 = 1a = a$"},
		{"id":"192bef7f18255231","x":335,"y":1160,"width":430,"height":137,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A commutative ring is a ring $(R, +, \\cdot)$ further satisfying\n1) $\\forall a, b \\in R, \\enspace ab = ba$"},
		{"id":"ce92542f110ff761","x":344,"y":1340,"width":413,"height":165,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A field $F$ is a triple $(F, +, \\cdot)$ where $F$ is a set, and $+, \\cdot$ are binary operations satisfying\n1) $(F, +, \\cdot)$ is a commutative ring\n2) $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an abelian group"},
		{"id":"971bc9099ecf17cf","x":313,"y":1560,"width":476,"height":326,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A vector space $V$ over a field $F$ is a set $V$ with two binary operations\n$$+: V \\times V \\rightarrow V$$\n$$\\cdot: F \\times V \\rightarrow V$$\nsatisfying $\\forall a, b \\in F, \\forall \\mathbf{u, v} \\in V$:\n1) $(V, +)$ is an abelian group\n2) $a(\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = a\\mathbf{u} + a\\mathbf{v}$\n3) $(a + b) \\mathbf{v} = a\\mathbf{v} + b \\mathbf{v}$\n4) $(ab) \\mathbf{v} = a(b\\mathbf{v})$\n5) $1\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}$"},
		{"id":"3ecd63699b293e38","x":261,"y":1940,"width":580,"height":277,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. A complex Euclidean space is a vector space over the field $\\mathbb{C}$, where the set of vectors is a set of functions\n$$\\mathbb{C}^{\\Sigma} = \\{f : f: \\Sigma \\rightarrow \\mathbb{C}\\}$$\nand $\\forall u, v \\in \\mathbb{C}^{\\Sigma}, \\forall a \\in \\Sigma, \\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$\n1) $(u + v)(a) = u(a) + v(a)$\n2) $(\\alpha u)(a) = \\alpha u(a)$\n$$$$"},
		{"id":"79606298a0003f65","x":-400,"y":1520,"width":589,"height":220,"type":"text","text":"**Definition**. An instruction set $\\mathcal{G}$ for a $d$-dimensional qudit is a finite set of quantum gates satisfying:\n1) $\\forall g \\in \\mathcal{G}, \\enspace g \\in SU(d)$\n2) $\\forall g \\in \\mathcal{G}, \\enspace g^{-1} = g^{\\dagger} \\in \\mathcal{G}$\n3) $\\forall U \\in SU(d), \\forall \\epsilon \\gt 0, \\enspace \\exists g_1 \\dots g_m \\in \\mathcal{G}: \\| U - g_1 \\dots g_m \\|_{\\text{op}} \\leq \\epsilon$ "}
	],
